+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more groups of a whole number followed by a unit: `d` for days, `h` for
+hours, `m` for minutes, and `s` for seconds. Each unit can be used at most once, in that order, without spaces. Units
+are case-insensitive, so `5400`, `90m`, `1h30m`, and `1H30M` all mean the same duration. Negative numbers and
+decimals are not accepted. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` value, such as a default, is passed through unchanged.
+
+In help output, the value is shown with the metavar `DURATION`.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="1h30m", show_default=True)
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=2m30s'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=2 minutes'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.5.1
+```
+
